        <div class="work-tags">
          <span class="work-tag">#Muslim-world</span><span class="work-tag">#postcolonialism</span>
          <span class="work-tag">#GANEFO</span><span class="work-tag">#Islamic-Games</span><span class="work-tag">#gender</span>
        </div>
        <div class="work-abstract">
          <p>Published in Brown University's prestigious policy journal, this article investigates how modern sport — as a product of Western modernity — both shapes and reflects transformations in the Muslim world. Three illustrative case studies frame the argument: (1) sport and nation-state building in the postcolonial era, from GANEFO (1963) to Gulf commercialisation; (2) the condition of women in Muslim societies through the lens of Muslim women's Olympic participation; and (3) the inaugural 2005 Islamic Solidarity Games as a reassertion of Islamic identity in global sport. The article argues that the Muslim world's engagement with sport is not a simple story of Westernisation, but a complex negotiation between global and local, modernity and authenticity.</p>
          <div class="significance">
            <strong>Why it matters</strong>
            One of the few academic analyses of sport in the Muslim world published in an international affairs journal — bridging sport studies with political science and area studies audiences. It positions sport as a domain of geopolitical significance, not merely cultural practice.
