        <div class="work-title">Veiled Women Athletes in the 2008 Beijing Olympics: Media Accounts</div>
        <div class="work-venue"><em>International Journal of the History of Sport</em>, Vol. 29, No. 4, pp. 638–651 · DOI: 10.1080/09523367.2012.658194</div>
        <div class="work-tags">
          <span class="work-tag">#Beijing2008</span><span class="work-tag">#hijab</span>
          <span class="work-tag">#gender</span><span class="work-tag">#Olympics</span>
          <span class="work-tag">#media-analysis</span><span class="work-tag">#Orientalism</span>
        </div>
        <div class="work-abstract">
          <p>This paper undertakes a comparative analysis of international media accounts — Western (English, French), Arab (Arabic), Iranian, and Chinese — regarding approximately 14 veiled Muslim athletes who competed in Beijing 2008. Using inductive thematic analysis, the paper identifies four dominant discourses: (1) Western secular media framing the veil as incompatible with Olympic values; (2) Iranian and Arab media celebrating veiled athletes as evidence of Muslim women's agency; (3) the "commercialised hijab" debate generated by Roqaya Al-Ghasara's Australian-designed "hijood"; (4) Chinese media using the Games as a narrative of civilisational bridge-building. The paper critically deploys Orientalism theory to challenge the Western media's condescension toward Muslim women's autonomous choices.</p>
          <div class="significance">
            <strong>Why it matters</strong>
            A methodologically distinctive work — one of the few comparative multilingual media analyses in sport studies. It challenges both Western secular universalism and Islamist restrictions on women's bodies, positioning the author as a genuinely cross-cultural scholarly voice.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
