        <div class="work-title">Sport, Islam, and Muslims in Europe: In Between or on the Margin?</div>
        <div class="work-venue"><em>Religions</em> (MDPI), Vol. 4, No. 4, pp. 644–656 · Published 10 December 2013 · DOI: 10.3390/rel4040644</div>
        <div class="work-tags">
          <span class="work-tag">#Islam</span><span class="work-tag">#Europe</span>
          <span class="work-tag">#France</span><span class="work-tag">#identity</span>
          <span class="work-tag">#laïcité</span><span class="work-tag">#reverse-migration</span>
          <span class="work-tag">#Orientalism</span><span class="work-tag">#post-secular</span>
        </div>
        <div class="work-abstract">
          <p>Using Arkoun's concept of "the crisis of meanings," this article examines how misconceptions about Islam's role in Europe distort the discourse on sport, immigration, and integration. France serves as the principal case study — the country where debates on secularism (<em>laïcité</em>), Muslim identity, and republican values are most contentious. The paper is structured across three analytical layers: (1) the religious phenomenon in Europe, drawing on Touraine's distinction between <em>communautarisme</em> (self-segregation) and legitimate "cultural rights"; Habermas's notion of the "post-secular" society; and Arkoun's critique of the Western "culture of disbelief"; (2) a typology of Muslim identity positions in Europe — from Ritualised and Nationalist Islam to Neo-reformist and Secular — providing a sophisticated framework for understanding Muslim heterogeneity; and (3) a series of sport incidents in France used as prisms for the "crisis of meanings": Zidane's headbutt at the 2006 World Cup (read through Orientalist frames of "irrationality" and "Muslim masculinity"); the booing of La Marseillaise at the France–Tunisia friendly (2008); and the halal meals controversy during the 2010 World Cup. The paper also analyses the "reverse migration" phenomenon — North African-origin athletes in Europe choosing to represent Algeria, Tunisia, or Morocco — as a terrain of contested loyalties on both sides of the Mediterranean.</p>
          <div class="significance">
            <strong>Why it matters</strong>
            One of the few academic papers to apply a theoretically sophisticated European sociology of religion framework (Habermas, Touraine, Arkoun, Said) directly to the sport domain. It moves beyond both assimilationist and multiculturalist frameworks to show how sport functions as a politicised space where Muslim athletes are held to impossible double standards of loyalty — in their countries of birth and their countries of origin simultaneously. The Zidane, Marseillaise, and halal incidents remain touchstone cases in the sociology of sport and Islam in Europe.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

