        <div class="work-abstract">
          <p>Co-authored with Professor Ian P. Henry (Loughborough University), this chapter appears in a Palgrave volume on Islam and multiculturalism in the West. It critically deconstructs the framing of Muslim communities in British sport policy and welfare discourse, drawing on empirical case studies from Leicester and Birmingham — two cities with significant Muslim populations. The chapter challenges reductive representations of Muslim sporting needs as a "problem" to be managed, arguing instead for a framework that recognises the complex, heterogeneous, and locally specific character of Muslim communities' relationship with sport, leisure, and public services. It connects to the broader chapter's wider argument about sport as a site where the integration debate is tested and contested.</p>
          <div class="significance">
            <strong>Why it matters</strong>
            Brings together the theoretical framework developed in the <em>Religions</em> 2013 article with empirical evidence from British Muslim communities, contributing directly to sport welfare and sport policy debates in a multicultural European context. It is a key reference for practitioners and researchers working on inclusion, diversity, and sport provision for minority communities.
          </div>
        </div>
