        <div class="work-abstract">
          <p>Written in the wake of the 2011 Arab Spring, this chapter examines sport as a contested terrain during Tunisia's political transition under the Islamist party Ennahda (2012–2014). The author analyses the 2012 London Olympics as a test case: gold medallist Oussama El-Melouli was targeted by a Salafi Jihadi group for drinking water during Ramadan; silver medallist Habiba Ghribi faced calls to have her citizenship stripped for her "indecent" athletic attire. The chapter traces how Tunisia's secular-nationalist sport heritage, built under Bourguiba and Ben Ali, became a battlefield between Islamist and secular movements — with the bodies of female athletes as the central site of contestation.</p>
          <div class="significance">
            <strong>Why it matters</strong>
            A rare primary-source analysis of the intersection of sport, religion, and post-revolutionary politics in North Africa. The chapter demonstrates how sport functions as a barometer of political transition — and how women athletes' bodies become symbolic battlegrounds in ideological struggles.
          </div>
