        <div class="work-abstract">
          <p>This edited volume brings together 14 contributions from scholars across sport sociology, anthropology, Islamic studies, and political science to examine sport in Muslim-majority countries and among Muslim minorities in the West. Structured across four parts — Understanding Sport in Islam; Gender, Body and Culture; Sport and Politics of Identity; Sport Development and Sport for Development — the volume offers the most comprehensive comparative treatment of its subject to date. Chapters cover Germany, Norway, Turkey, Palestine, Tunisia, Malaysia, the UK, and Italy. Amara co-authored the Introduction and Conclusion, and contributed the Tunisia chapter.</p>
          <div class="significance">
            <strong>Why it matters</strong>
            The first systematic comparative edited volume on sport and Islam across both Muslim-majority and Muslim-minority contexts. Published by the world's leading sport studies publisher, it became a standard reference in the field and is widely assigned in graduate courses in sport sociology, Islamic studies, and migration studies.
