        <div class="work-abstract">
          <p>This chapter examines the emerging field of "Islamic marketing" in relation to sport, interrogating whether marketing — rooted in free-market capitalism and consumerism — can be genuinely reconciled with Islamic ethics. The chapter surveys halal/haram distinctions in sport sponsorship (alcohol, gambling, usury), analyses case studies including Frederic Kanoute's refusal to wear a gambling sponsor's shirt, Papiss Cissé's dispute with Newcastle FC over the Wonga logo, and Qatar's dilemma over beer sponsorship at the 2022 FIFA World Cup. It also examines the growing halal beer market as a paradox of Islamic consumerism.</p>
          <div class="significance">
            <strong>Why it matters</strong>
            Provides the first theoretical framework for understanding sport marketing in Muslim contexts — a gap that became commercially urgent as Gulf states invested billions in global sport and as the Muslim consumer market reached 1.8 billion people. Directly relevant to practitioners in sport management and brand strategy.
